#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:4
#: 048b7b4c08014c459f0de0dd03dbffc5
msgid "Building a QuadPlane"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:6
#: bc487831f02344628ef8575030cec275
msgid "Putting together a QuadPlane involves careful planning. This page will give you some general guidance on design principles for QuadPlanes that may help you with your build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:14
#: 5cc28d12317246dcaeef10b09cdfa5e5
msgid "General Rules"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:16
#: 5c6dba33e564406796dd85790b1a0047
msgid "A wide range of fixed wing aircraft can be converted to have VTOL capabilities. While ArduPilot uses the name QuadPlane for these aircraft, you are not restricted to just QuadCopter motor layouts. Almost any multicopter motor arrangement can be used with a QuadPlane, including quad, hexa, tricoper, octa and octaquad. In addition, tilting motors to allow their use in both VTOL and Fixed Wing flight is possible. Finally, versions which use a vertical stance (nose vertical) for VTOL operations are possible."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:25
#: bbd4d8e117624c9886c79d245ecffdb9
msgid "Some of the key factors to success are:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:27
#: 374e0a3f9a5d480fa990863ca75ae524
msgid "a fixed wing frame that can carry the weight of the additional lifting motors and power system, along with any payload needed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:30
#: 4593249092e74f3fab5cbaeecdc95ef7
msgid "sufficient power in the lifting motors not just for the total airframe weight, but also for the additional load that may be induced by downforce on the wings for configurations using a horizontal VTOL stance."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:34
#: c2ddfee393b142059c41a7957bc99424
msgid "complete clearance above and below the whole disk area of the lifting motors, to ensure they achieve full aerodynamic thrust"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:37
#: 49f260d78b654c61a0edcb2fc477e8da
msgid "minimum wing, frame, and mount twist and flex so the motors provide thrust vertically at all times"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:40
#: c1ae1261dc7c4438bbdeacc4dcf0bba7
msgid "a sufficiently robust mounting system for the lifting motors"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:42
#: cd71fa65b2984e2787dd65b6060524b4
msgid "minimizing aerodynamic drag from the lifting motors and frame"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:47
#: 32175b74df944a688a1eb86f74ae8005
msgid "When you are designing a QuadPlane it is highly recommended that you make use of `eCalc <http://ecalc.ch/>`__ to help choose the motors, ESCs, batteries, propellers and other components of your design. Brushless motors vary a lot in their power to weight ratio, and ensuring you choose motors that keep the weight down while supplying sufficient lifting power is important."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:54
#: dcde08fcc2c94f558a23ff2ffb9dcc5d
msgid "Due to their greater mass and surface area, most QuadPlanes do not have as robust YAW authority as compared to small quadcopters (unless vectored thrust is employed with tilt rotors). It is extremely important that the motors be very well aligned, as just a few degrees offset can sometimes effectively eliminate the ability to yaw in one or both directions. In fact, this effect can be used to actually increase yaw authority by purposely tilting one or both pairs of adjacent rotating motors in the natural direction of their torque 1 or 2 degrees (inward for H frame, outward for X and + frames)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:57
#: 136358ffc290433fb1e5d8f28f5d2655
msgid "QuadPlane Range"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:59
#: 35ad0d03799146ba878b184cc2ca7c6e
msgid "Perhaps surprisingly, it is sometimes possible to increase the potential range of an aircraft using a QuadPlane conversion. This may seem counter intuitive as a QuadPlane conversion will both add weight and increase aerodynamic drag to an airframe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:64
#: 0484ef03c0aa4a9ab4b86dae101dce71
msgid "The reason why range can be increased is the extra carrying capacity of a QuadPlane. Many fixed wing aircraft are limited in the amount of battery they can carry due to the requirements for reliable launch. During launch, and especially when using a flying launch such as a catapult or bungee, the aircraft needs to rapidly accelerate to an airspeed above its stall speed. If it fails to reach that speed sufficiently quickly then it will crash. A QuadPlane avoids this problem by taking off vertically, and can spend longer on the acceleration needed to sufficient speed for forward flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:74
#: b13092ecc52e47e280bb2392de6dd13d
msgid "This means it is often possible to pack a lot more battery into a QuadPlane than is possible in the same airframe without VTOL motors. The extra battery capacity can more than make up for the increased weight and drag of the VTOL motors."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:79
#: 8a657699f23041ef9430538f4c17896f
msgid "To make the most of this advantage you need to do very rapid VTOL takeoffs and landings to minimise the battery consumption in VTOL flight. The video below demonstrates just how rapid a takeoff can be achieved with a properly setup quadplane."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:87
#: 23dfbb756e374421827958699a21207e
msgid "A second factor that can help with QuadPlane range is the flexibility available in choosing the propeller and power train for the forward motor. As conventional takeoff is not needed the forward motor does not need to be optimised for the high level of thrust needed for takeoff. This can allow larger propellers and geared motors to be used that are highly efficient for forward cruise flight."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/quadplane-building.rst:94
#: 377ced9d4cfa4949be1b9557e4b3f5ed
msgid "Finally, for really long range with a QuadPlane you can use an internal combustion engine for the forward motor. A gas engine can run for a lot longer than an electric motor with the same weight of fuel."
msgstr ""

